Do not read DB_CONFIG from the current working directory (CVE-2017-10140)

Berkeley DB reads the configuration file DB_CONFIG from the current working directory when an
environment is opened without a home directory, e.g. by db_create() with dbenv == NULL - and with
it settings an untrusted user can place there. With this patch, DB_CONFIG is read only from the
home directory of the environment.

Origin: Fedora/Red Hat libdb, db-5.3.28-cwd-db_config.patch (Red Hat bug 1464032), also used by
Debian as CVE-2017-10140-cwd-db_config.patch. Under the license of Berkeley DB 5.3.28.
Applied by cmake/BuildThirdParty.cmake.

--- a/src/env/env_open.c
+++ b/src/env/env_open.c
@@ -473,7 +473,7 @@
 	env->db_mode = mode == 0 ? DB_MODE_660 : mode;
 
 	/* Read the DB_CONFIG file. */
-	if ((ret = __env_read_db_config(env)) != 0)
+	if (env->db_home != NULL && (ret = __env_read_db_config(env)) != 0)
 		return (ret);
 
 	/*
